With `strict` TypeScript and React 19 types, which line fails to compile?

```tsx
function Card({ children }: { children: ReactElement }) {
  return <div className="card">{children}</div>;
}
function Panel({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  return <section>{children}</section>;
}

const a = <Panel>Hello</Panel>;
const b = <Panel>{null}</Panel>;
const c = <Card>Hello</Card>;
const d = <Card><p>Hello</p></Card>;
```

A handler is declared outside the JSX so it can be reused. Which parameter type compiles under `strict` and lets you read `e.target.value`?

```tsx
function onChange(e: /* ? */) {
  setName(e.target.value);
}
<input onChange={onChange} />
```

In a `.tsx` file, this generic component fails to parse ("JSX element 'T' has no corresponding closing tag"). What is the smallest fix?

```tsx
export const List = <T>(props: { items: T[]; render: (item: T) => ReactNode }) => (
  <ul>{props.items.map(props.render)}</ul>
);
```

After upgrading to React 19 and @types/react 19, this line fails with "Cannot find namespace 'JSX'". What is the right fix?

```tsx
export function Badge(): JSX.Element {
  return <span className="badge" />;
}
```

This compiles in JavaScript but not in strict TypeScript. Why, and what is the fix?

```tsx
const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
// later, after loading:
setUser({ id: 1, name: 'Ann' });
```

Using React 19's TypeScript types, which statement about this component is correct?

```tsx
function Search() {
  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);
  const timer = useRef<number>();
  useEffect(() => { inputRef.current.focus(); }, []);
  return <input ref={inputRef} />;
}
```

A design-system input should accept every native `<input>` prop, plus a custom `size` of `'sm' | 'lg'`. With version 1 below, `<TextField size="sm" />` fails to compile. What is going on?

```tsx
// version 1
type Props = ComponentProps<'input'> & { size: 'sm' | 'lg' };
// version 2
type Props = Omit<ComponentProps<'input'>, 'size'> & { size: 'sm' | 'lg' };
```

With these props, which usage or implementation fails to compile under `strict`?

```tsx
type ActionProps =
  | { as: 'link'; href: string }
  | { as: 'button'; onClick: () => void };

function Action(props: ActionProps) {
  return props.as === 'link'
    ? <a href={props.href}>Go</a>
    : <button onClick={props.onClick}>Go</button>;
}
```

An API client does `const user = (await res.json()) as User`. One day the API returns `{ "id": "7", "name": "Ann" }`, with the id as a string. What changes if you parse with Zod instead?

```ts
const User = z.object({ id: z.number(), name: z.string() });
const result = User.safeParse(await res.json());
```

With React Router, `/settings/profile` matches the nested route below, but only the settings sidebar renders; the profile page never appears. What is missing?

```jsx
<Route path="settings" element={<SettingsLayout />}>
  <Route path="profile" element={<Profile />} />
</Route>

function SettingsLayout() {
  return <aside>Settings menu</aside>;
}
```

A Vite + React project uses CSS Modules with Vite's default settings. The stylesheet defines `.btn-primary { … }`, but the button below gets no class. Why?

```jsx
import styles from './Button.module.css';

export function Button() {
  return <button className={styles.btnPrimary}>Save</button>;
}
```

In a Tailwind CSS project, badges built with this code have no background colour in production, although `bg-red-500` works elsewhere. Why?

```jsx
function Badge({ color }) {
  return <span className={`bg-${color}-500 px-2`}>New</span>;
}
```

In a MobX app, clicking "Add" pushes to `store.items`, but `TodoList` never re-renders, even though logging shows the array has grown. What is the likely cause?

```jsx
function TodoList() {
  return <ul>{store.items.map(t => <li key={t.id}>{t.text}</li>)}</ul>;
}
```

A web developer writes their first React Native screen. Which line causes the error "Text strings must be rendered within a `<Text>` component"?

```jsx
export function Welcome({ name }) {
  return (
    <View style={styles.box}>
      <Image source={logo} />
      Hello, {name}
      <Text>Welcome back</Text>
    </View>
  );
}
